   emits. Section 8 below is the one desktop layout, and it is no longer
   scoped to .veloura-top-logo-centered either, so turning the mobile toggle
   off can never drop the desktop header back to the raw template again.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   10.3 SEARCH, LANGUAGE AND LOGIN — ONE MATERIAL, ONE HEIGHT, ONE TEXT COLOUR

   Three separate faults were measured here, and each needed its own fix.

   (a) HEIGHT. The search bar was sized by --veloura-search-height (36px) and
       the two pills by --veloura-icon-hit (38px), so the row never lined up.
       All three now read one token, --veloura-control-h, and the icon rail is
       given the same number so the whole row shares a single baseline.

   (b) COLOUR. --veloura-site-bg is declared ONCE, at :root in master.twig,
       from the light background setting. It does not change in dark mode, so
       "take the store background" was painting the LIGHT colour on a dark
       page. The store's real background is --veloura-site-bg in light and
       --veloura-dark-body-bg in dark, and those are aliased here into a pair
       of tokens that do switch.

       The same applied to the text. --veloura-top-text is the HEADER text
       setting, and this store has it set to a near-white, which is correct
       over the old coloured header and invisible over a light store
       background. The controls no longer wear the header's colour; they wear
       the store's own text colour, which switches with the theme.

   (c) EDGE. Glass wants a hairline, but a full border round a pill reads as a
       button, and so does a dark bottom edge — that is a drop shadow drawn as
       a line, and it makes the control look raised rather than see-through.
       Both edges are therefore LIGHT in both themes: white at very low alpha
       over the dark surface, silver over the light one, with the bottom
       fainter than the top so the shape still has an implied light source.
       Nothing is drawn at all when the glass option is off.

       The fill is carried at 22% of the store colour, not 46%, so the panel
       and whatever is scrolling behind it read through the control; the blur
       is raised to 18px to keep that from turning into noise.

       The store colour ALONE is not enough to see. Measured over the dark
       header: the control landed at luminance 19 against a panel at 28 — the
       store's dark background is darker than the panel it sits on, so a
       translucent wash of it makes the control recede instead of reading as a
       separate pane, and in light the two were 232 against 229, which is no
       difference at all. Glass is legible because it catches light, so a thin
       white veil is laid over the store tint: enough to lift the control
       clear of the panel, far too little to make it opaque. The veil is what
       is tuned per theme — .10 over dark, .55 over light — because the light
       theme starts much closer to white and needs more of it to move.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
/* The switching tokens. Both are new names, so neither can be
   self-referential: --veloura-control-surface never reads itself. */
html body {
  --veloura-control-h: 40px;
  --veloura-control-surface: var(--veloura-site-bg, #f4f5f7);
  --veloura-control-text: var(--veloura-primary-text, var(--color-text, #111827));
  /* Light: one silver hairline, top a little stronger than the bottom.
     Neither edge is a shadow — a dark bottom edge reads as a raised button,
     which is the opposite of glass. */
  --veloura-control-edge-top: rgba(203, 213, 225, .95);
  --veloura-control-edge-bottom: rgba(203, 213, 225, .50);
  /* See the veil note in (b) below. */
  --veloura-control-veil: rgba(255, 255, 255, .55);
}
html.dark body,
html body.dark {
  --veloura-control-surface: var(--veloura-dark-body-bg, #010202);
  --veloura-control-text: var(--veloura-dark-primary-text, #ffffff);
  /* Dark: BOTH edges are light. Sampled off the reference: the line sits about
     40 luminance above the surface on top and about 30 below that on the
     bottom, i.e. roughly .18 and .13 alpha white. Taken a shade further down
     because the ask was for the faintest possible lip. */
  --veloura-control-edge-top: rgba(255, 255, 255, .16);
  --veloura-control-edge-bottom: rgba(255, 255, 255, .07);
  --veloura-control-veil: rgba(255, 255, 255, .13);
}
/* The icon rail is measured with the same number, so the row has one baseline.

   TWO tokens size the search, not one: --veloura-search-height (declared in
   header-glass-search.scss) drives the bar itself, and
   --veloura-header-search-height (declared in BOTH header-search.scss and
   header-submenu.scss) drives .veloura-search-surface, where it is applied as
   max-height. Setting only the first one left the max-height capping the bar
   at 36px while everything else had already moved to 40 — measured, not
   assumed. Both are redirected to the one number. */
@media (min-width: 1024px) {
  html body .veloura-header-tabs-stack .store-header.veloura-top-enabled {
    --veloura-icon-hit: var(--veloura-control-h, 40px);
    --veloura-search-height: var(--veloura-control-h, 40px);
    --veloura-header-search-height: var(--veloura-control-h, 40px);
  }
}
