html body .veloura-product-page #btn-show-more.veloura-product-read-more {
  position: absolute !important;
  left: 50% !important;
  right: auto !important;
  bottom: 0 !important;
  margin: 0 !important;
  transform: translate(-50%, 50%) !important;
  z-index: 6 !important;
  background: #414042 !important;
  background: var(--veloura-product-primary, var(--color-primary, #004d65)) !important;
  color: #ffffff !important;
  border: 6px solid #ffffff !important;
  border: 6px solid var(--veloura-product-basic-bg, #ffffff) !important;
  box-shadow: none !important;
}
html body .veloura-product-page #btn-show-more.veloura-product-read-more {
  /* CENTRED ON THE EDGE, not near it.

     product.scss contains two rules for this button with the same selector:
     an earlier one that pins it to the edge, and a later one that puts it
     back into normal flow and pulls it down with a negative margin. The later
     one wins, and a negative margin can only approximate the edge — it has to
     guess the button's height, so the button ends up sitting just inside the
     panel instead of straddling its edge.

     Anchoring to the bottom of the panel and shifting by half its own height
     puts the centre exactly on the edge, whatever the button measures. */
  /* THE STORE'S PRIMARY COLOUR, IN BOTH STATES.

     Measured before this change: collapsed the button was painted with the
     panel colour but its text was already white — white on a light panel,
     which is why the label was so hard to read. Expanded, a different rule
     switched it to the primary colour. So the two states disagreed, and one
     of them was unreadable.

     One colour for both states fixes the contrast and matches the store's
     identity: primary fill, white label, and the ring in the page colour so
     the button still reads as cut out of the panel edge. */
}
